  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals the Upper Great Lakes \non the northern fringes of a \"Ring of Fire\" upper level ridge. \nSubtle shortwaves rotating around the top of the ridge will be the \nfocal point for rounds of strong to severe thunderstorms expected \ntoday through parts of Monday before building surface high pressure \nreturns \"quieter\" weather to the region into the midweek.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will carry a mid-level impulse into the region \nthis afternoon/evening, driving strong to severe thunderstorms from \nMN into Superior/UP and initiating additional storms. Increased \nascent ahead of a midlevel impulse will help erode a strong cap \ncurrently atop the UP. Be the time the cap erodes, a fairly volatile \nenvironment will be supportive of strong to severe thunderstorms. CAM \nensembles suggest healthy mucape upwards of 1500-2500 j/kg with bulk \nshear values pushing 45-55 kts. Low level soundings across the \ninterior central and west show fairly dry low levels and high LCLs \nwith rather straight line hodographs between 1-6 km, pointing to \nmore of a hail/wind threat. Still, 0-1 km SRH pushes 50-150 m2/s2 at \ntimes, so if an isolated supercell can tap into ample surface based \ninstability, cant rule out an isolated tornado. Latest RAP analysis \nsuggests between 1000-1500 DCAPE already in place across the west \nhalf, thus air parcels will have plenty of downward momentum to \ncreate potentially damaging winds, especially if isolated supercells \ncan merge and grow upscale into more linear segments. The main issue \nis timing out convection. CAMs are still struggling on the spatial \nextent and specific timing of storms this afternoon/evening, but the \nbulk of CAM members suggest activity reaching the western UP/lake \nbetween 5-7 pm edt carrying southeast through midnight. This morning \nSPC has introduced a wind/hail driven Enhanced Risk (level 3 of 5) \nfor severe weather across all of WI, including parts of our border \ncounties. Elsewhere, a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) remains for most \nof the UP. Needless to say, those with outdoor plans should remain \nweather aware tonight. Have a way to receive a warning and a plan \nshould one be issued!\n\nAnother round of strong to severe storms is possible tomorrow as a \ndeeper shortwave dives through the Great Lakes. SPC continues to \nplace most of the UP in a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5), though there \nremains a fair amount of uncertainty with this round as well, and it \nmay have some contingency on how storm behavior occurs tonight. CAM \nguidance is not as excited about severe weather parameters, nor does \nit have any handle on timing/spatial coverage of precipitation.\n\nBehind the Clipper low on Monday, expect quieter weather across the \nregion as geopotential heights rise over the central CONUS into \nCanada. With a semi-omega block appearance forming over the central \nCONUS, we could see a few weak Canadian shortwaves move across the \narea (mainly the first weekend of August). However, medium-range \nruns are showing mid-level heights increasing locally over the Upper \nMidwest and Great Lakes for the middle of the week, thinking we will \nmore likely than not remain dry until potentially next weekend.",
